Now I understand the vulnerability. In `_cupsRasterExecPS`, when handling `CUPS_PS_COPY`:

```c
case CUPS_PS_COPY :
    pop_stack(st);   // pop the "copy" operator
    if ((obj = pop_stack(st)) != NULL)  // pop the count
    {
      copy_stack(st, (int)obj->value.number);
      ...
    }
    break;
```

The issue: `obj` points into `st->objs` (the stack array). When `copy_stack` is called, it calls `push_stack` which may call `realloc` on `st->objs`. If `realloc` moves the memory, then `obj` (which points into the old `st->objs` buffer) becomes a dangling pointer. But in this case, `obj` is only used for `obj->value.number` before `copy_stack` is called, so that's fine.

Wait, let me re-read. The crash is a heap-use-after-free READ in `copy_stack`. Let me look at `copy_stack` again:

```c
copy_stack(_cups_ps_stack_t *st, int c)
{
  int n;
  if (c < 0) return (-1);
  else if (c == 0) return (0);
  if ((n = st->num_objs - c) < 0) return (-1);
  while (c > 0)
  {
    if (!push_stack(st, st->objs + n))  // <-- HERE
      return (-1);
    n ++;
    c --;
  }
  return (0);
}
```

The bug: `st->objs + n` is computed, then `push_stack` is called. Inside `push_stack`, if `st->num_objs >= st->alloc_objs`, it calls `realloc(st->objs, ...)`. If realloc moves the buffer, `st->objs` is updated to the new location, but the argument `st->objs + n` was already evaluated using the OLD pointer. So `push_stack` copies from the freed old buffer → heap-use-after-free READ.

So the trigger is: `copy` with a count `c` such that `st->num_objs - c >= 0` (so it passes the check), but `st->num_objs >= st->alloc_objs` (so realloc happens). Actually, `push_stack` reallocs when `num_objs >= alloc_objs`. 

